+# Decision: Concurrency mechanism for independently edited visits
+
+## Context
+
+This decision is owned by the record-store node under the HLD's delegation of the concurrency mechanism, and builds on the choice to store each submission as a whole visit rather than as field-level changes.
+
+A coastal conservation team surveys about 6,000 plot visits per season with 24 seasonal workers sharing 12 offline-capable tablets. Workers sometimes correct the same visit independently after comparing field notes, and corrections can arrive days after the starting state was fetched. Two coordinators resolve questions, and a regional science partner receives a reviewed CSV every Friday. The concurrency mechanism decides what a submission carries, what review work looks like, and which visit states may enter an export.
+
+What is at stake: a current record whose fields were never jointly observed by any worker would be scientifically meaningless to the partner, and a review queue that leaves too many visits unresolved would hold rows out of the Friday CSV. Every mechanism under consideration retains original submissions and never discards a submitted edit; that invariant is a standing HLD tenet and a background constraint, not a point of difference between the options.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+The elements below are the load-bearing criteria, with importance marked. Both options satisfy the standing no-discard and offline-capture constraints, so no option is eliminated by a gate; the choice is a weighing of graded criteria.
+
+- **Scientific integrity of the current record — major.** An automatically combined record can join a species identification from one worker with a cover estimate from another, producing a value no worker submitted together. Field names do not reveal which observations are scientifically related, so a rule set cannot reliably detect the combination. Derived from the tenets *Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable* and *Do not silently discard a submitted edit*.
+- **Coordinator workload and operability — major.** Two coordinators must absorb the review queue. Derived from *Prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator*.
+- **Export completeness against the weekly deadline — moderate.** Visits with unresolved review work are held out of the partner's Friday CSV; a mechanism that generates more unresolved visits puts more rows at risk. Derived from *Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable*.
+- **Implementation and maintenance burden — moderate.** Merge logic that must be maintained as the schema grows needs ongoing engineering care. Derived from *Prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator*.
+- **Offline submission size and tablet state — minor.** What a tablet must retain and resend while disconnected. Derived from *Let field workers continue collecting observations without connectivity*.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-visit revisions
+
+**Description.** Each submission is a complete, immutable snapshot of the visit naming the base revision the worker started from. The server never splits a visit into fields. A submission whose base is no longer the current tip becomes a competing, intact revision; a coordinator resolves the set by selecting among them. Every exported value is an intact submitted visit a human reviewed. The only machinery is revision lineage, comparison, and resolution — there are no merge rules and no field-level provenance in the current record.
+
+**Demonstration.** Two workers fetch revision V1 of plot 12; one corrects an observer name, the other adds a missing species with its cover estimate. Both submit with base V1. The store keeps both submissions intact as competing revisions; a coordinator compares and selects one. Neither field change is silently combined.
+
+**Analysis**
+- Scientific integrity: **strong** — no automatic combination exists; every current value was jointly submitted and reviewed.
+- Coordinator workload: **weak** — even unrelated-field edits compete, so review volume is maximal.
+- Export completeness: **weak** — a larger queue can leave more visits unresolved at the Friday export.
+- Implementation and maintenance burden: **strong** — no merge rules to author or maintain; two coordinators can operate it.
+- Offline submission size and tablet state: **strong** — tablets track base revision IDs and resend whole visits; no base snapshots or structured change sets.
+
+**Summary.** Trades review volume and export completeness for inspectability and the absence of maintained merge logic.
+
+### Field-aware three-way merge
+
+**Description.** Each submission carries its base snapshot and a structured set of field-level changes. The server combines changes to disjoint fields automatically — with merge rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries — and sends only overlapping changes to review. Field-level provenance records which submission supplied each field. Most visits become export-ready without coordinator action, so the review queue is limited to genuine conflicts and the partner's CSV is fuller.
+
+**Demonstration.** The same two workers: the observer-name correction and the new species target disjoint fields, so the server combines them into one current record with provenance and no review. Only a concurrent revision of the same species cover estimate would create review work.
+
+**Analysis**
+- Scientific integrity: **weak** — combining disjoint fields can cross scientific relationships the rules do not model; a species identification and its cover estimate are disjoint by field name but belong together, and a coordinator may not notice the resulting combination.
+- Coordinator workload: **strong** — only overlapping edits create review; far fewer visits enter the queue.
+- Export completeness: **strong** — more visits are resolved before the Friday export.
+- Implementation and maintenance burden: **weak** — merge rules for each field type plus provenance must be authored, tested, and maintained as the schema grows.
+- Offline submission size and tablet state: **partial** — tablets retain base snapshots and structured change sets, increasing local state and retry complexity.
+
+**Summary.** Reduces review volume and maximizes export completeness at the cost of maintained merge rules and the risk of silently joining scientifically related edits.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Chosen: whole-visit revisions.** It wins on the major criterion — scientific integrity of the current record — and turns on the tenets *Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable* and *Do not silently discard a submitted edit*. Field-aware three-way merge is genuinely stronger on coordinator workload and export completeness, but its weakness cannot be reliably engineered away: field names do not mark which observations are scientifically related, so a rule set can combine a species identification with its cover estimate and produce a record no worker submitted, with no coordinator noticing. A larger coordinator review queue, and the risk of more visits held out of the Friday CSV, are accepted; a mechanism that can silently combine scientifically related edits is not. The *Prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator* tenet reinforces the choice by removing maintained merge rules from the system.
+
+**Why field-aware three-way merge remains a live alternative.** It is not dismissed on capability: on the two graded criteria it leads, it would be the better mechanism under different domain conditions. A cold reader should re-run the judgment if (1) scientific dependencies between fields become explicitly modeled and machine-checkable, so a merge can refuse combinations that cross them; (2) coordinator capacity grows, or visit volume and the Friday deadline make the whole-visit review queue unworkable; or (3) a complete, testable merge-rule set with mandatory human approval of every combination removes the "without noticing" failure mode. Until one of those holds, the risk to scientific integrity outweighs the workload and export gains.
+
+**What this forecloses.** Automatic reduction of review volume, field-level provenance in the current record, and any maintained per-field merge logic are out of scope.
+
+**What it obligates downstream.** The decision's substance lives in record-store; the following are cascade obligations on sibling segments, not co-owned decisions. Those designs are written separately.
+
+- **tablet-sync** must retain whole-visit payloads rather than field deltas, track the base revision ID for each visit, retain local state across disconnection and retries, show field workers which competing revisions are pending, and explain server outcomes (accepted tip versus competing revision). Its local storage and retry state machine are sized around a submission envelope carrying the visit, the base revision ID, and an idempotency key — not around base snapshots and structured change sets.
+- **review-export** must provide a revision-comparison screen that presents competing intact revisions to a coordinator, and export-eligibility rules that hold any visit with unresolved competing revisions out of the partner's CSV. Its coordinator interface consumes the resolution outcome's contributing revision IDs. It does not need field-level provenance, since every current record is an intact submitted visit.
